{
  "result": "There is a special kind of magic in libraries on rainy afternoons, when the windows fog up and time seems to slow down just enough for you to hear your own thoughts thinking. I love wandering between tall shelves, trailing my fingers along spines, each one a doorway I haven't opened yet.\n\nIt doesn't really matter if I actually pick a book or just enjoy the possibility of picking one. The smell of paper and dust feels like comfort, like someone saved a seat for me across centuries. Outside, rain taps politely against glass, reminding me the world is still there, but not urgently needed.\n\nI think we all need places like that\u2014soft shelters from noise where curiosity is more important than productivity. Lately life feels so scheduled, so optimized, that even rest comes with a checklist. In a library, though, daydreaming counts as work. Wondering counts as progress. You can follow a footnote about whales, then suddenly learn about stars, then forget why you walked to that aisle at all, and it feels perfect.\n\nIf I could give everyone one gift, it would be that feeling: unhurried attention. To sit with a drink, watch rain make rivers on a windowpane, and let your mind wander without guilt. Maybe you'd think about summers, or a friend you miss, or a story you want to write. Whatever surfaces is welcome. Life is loud, but meaning often whispers. And sometimes, on a rainy afternoon, if you listen closely, you can hear it.",
